<title>BELGIUM: EU states reject cotton anti-dumping plan-diplomat.</title>
<headline>EU states reject cotton anti-dumping plan-diplomat.</headline>
<dateline>BRUSSELS 1997-05-15</dateline>
<text>
<p>European Union member states have rejected an anti-dumping plan proposed by the European Commission on imports of cotton from China, Egypt, India, Indonesia, Turkey and Pakistan, an EU diplomat said on Thursday.</p>
<p>&quot;It was rejected. There was a majority of states against,&quot; an EU diplomat told Reuters in reference to a European Commission proposal for duties on imports of unbleached cotton from the six countries.</p>
<p>The Commission imposed provisional duties against grey cotton fabrics from those countries last November, setting in train a further review period, which is due to expire on May 20, by which time duties become definitive or lapse.</p>
<p>Neither the EU nor other officials could say whether the file was now dead or whether the countries backing duties could patch together a majority before the deadline.</p>
<p>Belgium, Greece, Spain, France, Italy and Portugal backed the proposal on Thursday, two countries shy of the simple majority needed to impose final duties, the diplomat added.</p>
</text>
